1. Encapsulation
Wrapping data (variables) and methods into a single unit (class) is called Encapsulation.

Example: Using private variables and public getters and setters.
2. Polymorphism
One thing having many forms is called Polymorphism.

Example: Method overloading and method overriding.
3. Data Abstraction
Hiding internal implementation details and showing only essential information to the user is called Abstraction.

Example: Using abstract classes and interfaces.
4. Inheritance
One class acquiring the properties and methods of another class is called Inheritance.

Example: Using the extends keyword.

INHERITANCE
In Java, there are 5 types of inheritance.
1. Single Inheritance
One child class inherits from one parent class.

2. Multilevel Inheritance
One class inherits from another class, and a third class inherits from the second class.

3. Hierarchical Inheritance
Multiple child classes inherit from one parent class.

4. Multiple Inheritance
One child class inherits from multiple parent classes.

Important
 Java does not support multiple inheritance through classes, but it supports it through interfaces.
5. Hybrid Inheritance
Combination of two or more types of inheritance.

